1
00:00:00,272 --> 00:00:02,743
So we got a model file. How can we now build

2
00:00:02,759 --> 00:00:04,812
an actual model based on that file?

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:08,358
Well, for that, Ollama gives us the create

4
00:00:08,358 --> 00:00:08,743
command

5
00:00:09,657 --> 00:00:11,518
because as we learn here, this creates a

6
00:00:11,518 --> 00:00:13,299
model from a model file.

7
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:16,828
Now for that, you must make sure that in

8
00:00:16,876 --> 00:00:18,737
your command line, in your terminal, you

9
00:00:18,737 --> 00:00:21,031
navigate into the folder where you got that

10
00:00:21,031 --> 00:00:23,358
model file, which in my case is the case.

11
00:00:24,256 --> 00:00:26,101
And then you can run ollama create

12
00:00:26,964 --> 00:00:28,690
Then any name of your choice

13
00:00:29,626 --> 00:00:32,304
like service agent, but that is entirely up

14
00:00:32,304 --> 00:00:34,546
to you. That will be the name of the model

15
00:00:34,546 --> 00:00:35,159
you're creating.

16
00:00:36,298 --> 00:00:37,202
And then -f

17
00:00:38,059 --> 00:00:38,349
and then

18
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:42,581
./model_file. And here you simply specify

19
00:00:42,581 --> 00:00:44,310
the path to that model file.

20
00:00:45,406 --> 00:00:47,179
And since here for me it sits in the same

21
00:00:47,195 --> 00:00:49,581
directory, it's a very simple path. If you

22
00:00:49,613 --> 00:00:51,402
had it in some other directory, you could

23
00:00:51,402 --> 00:00:54,239
specify the full absolute path to that model

24
00:00:54,239 --> 00:00:54,698
file here.

25
00:00:56,610 --> 00:00:59,252
So now if you save that, it will build a new

26
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,905
model based on that model file.

27
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:04,603
And therefore, if thereafter you run ollama

28
00:01:04,603 --> 00:01:06,899
list, you should see that model here.

29
00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:11,040
Now again, this size is kind of wrong

30
00:01:11,153 --> 00:01:13,574
because of course this service agent model,

31
00:01:14,057 --> 00:01:16,136
thanks to this command we just executed,

32
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,328
is based on this model file, which builds a

33
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,083
model based on this Gemma model. And

34
00:01:21,083 --> 00:01:22,852
therefore, Ollama will not technically copy

35
00:01:22,852 --> 00:01:23,458
that model. It will not copy the weights.

36
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:27,823
Weights or parameters of that model. It will

37
00:01:27,936 --> 00:01:29,318
also not change them, by the way.

38
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:32,387
Instead, it will use that as a base and just

39
00:01:33,014 --> 00:01:36,043
adjust that metadata, so to say, so that

40
00:01:36,383 --> 00:01:39,085
these settings are always set when we now

41
00:01:39,376 --> 00:01:41,900
run our customized model. But under the

42
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,510
hood, we're still using the unchanged Gemma

43
00:01:44,576 --> 00:01:45,193
3 model.

44
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:49,246
But of course, therefore, here I can ollama

45
00:01:49,246 --> 00:01:50,724
run service agent.

46
00:01:52,119 --> 00:01:52,492
Like this,

47
00:01:53,902 --> 00:01:55,767
and this will now load my own model.

48
00:01:57,343 --> 00:01:59,865
And here we also see that chat history we

49
00:01:59,994 --> 00:02:01,239
added to that model.

50
00:02:02,015 --> 00:02:04,391
And if I type show system, I, for example,

51
00:02:04,456 --> 00:02:06,962
also see that system message which I added

52
00:02:06,962 --> 00:02:07,994
to that model file.

53
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:11,381
And that's therefore another, maybe more

54
00:02:11,397 --> 00:02:14,107
convenient way of building customized models

55
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:15,720
based on existing models,

56
00:02:16,559 --> 00:02:18,516
not by using set

57
00:02:19,142 --> 00:02:21,453
and save, which you can also do, which has

58
00:02:21,485 --> 00:02:22,304
the same effect,

59
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:25,482
but instead by using such a model file. And

60
00:02:25,498 --> 00:02:27,119
of course, the great thing about such a

61
00:02:27,119 --> 00:02:29,639
model file is that you can also share it

62
00:02:29,655 --> 00:02:31,405
with other people. You can upload it to

63
00:02:31,517 --> 00:02:33,748
GitHub, share it through your website, and

64
00:02:33,909 --> 00:02:36,365
therefore allow other people that are also

65
00:02:36,477 --> 00:02:40,345
using Ollama to easily copy your customized

66
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:41,212
model version.

67
00:02:42,015 --> 00:02:44,599
Now, for completeness sake, I also wanna

68
00:02:44,599 --> 00:02:46,413
mention that when it comes to sharing

69
00:02:46,589 --> 00:02:49,334
customized versions of models, you can also

70
00:02:49,398 --> 00:02:52,672
use the Ollama push command. And this pushes

71
00:02:52,785 --> 00:02:56,316
a model to a registry, a registry like the

72
00:02:56,316 --> 00:02:58,691
official Ollama models registry, for

73
00:02:58,723 --> 00:02:59,253
example.

74
00:03:00,248 --> 00:03:02,142
But that's not something I'll do in this

75
00:03:02,254 --> 00:03:04,020
course here, not something you have to do.

76
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:06,588
As I just explained, if you wanna share a

77
00:03:06,588 --> 00:03:08,610
customized version of a model, you could

78
00:03:08,738 --> 00:03:10,889
also just share the model file and have

79
00:03:11,001 --> 00:03:13,810
people create that model based on that model

80
00:03:13,810 --> 00:03:16,495
file. Locally on their systems, but it's

81
00:03:16,543 --> 00:03:19,309
worth noting that Ollama Push exists and can

82
00:03:19,357 --> 00:03:21,480
therefore be an interesting way of sharing

83
00:03:21,609 --> 00:03:23,860
an adjusted model with other people.
